## Advisory — radical innovation (not applied)

The plan's own pre-mortem names the one durable weakness and then accepts it: `RELATIONSHIPS` is a hand-maintained list inside the generator, so a future migration that adds a joinable table produces a diagram with a floating box and **nothing fails**. Drift moves from the column catalog (which step 6 deletes) into the generator — smaller and better-located, but still unguarded.

Concrete alternative: make each migration declare its own edges, and make missing declarations a CI failure.

- Adopt a comment convention in the migration SQL, e.g. `-- @rel messages(harness,session_id) -> sessions(harness,session_id)` beside the `CREATE TABLE`, plus `-- @rel-none` for a deliberately edge-free table (`_sync_state`) and repeated `@rel` lines for the polymorphic `embeddings.owner_table` case.
- The generator (still stdlib: `pathlib` + a regex over `skills/sr-search/src/stockroom/migrations/*.sql`) collects those declarations and keeps the golden snapshot as the entity/attribute source. Comments are invisible to DuckDB introspection, so the golden format never changes and the 0001 "no DB-level FOREIGN KEYs" invariant is untouched.
- Add one assertion: every entity in the head snapshot is either the target of an `@rel` or explicitly `@rel-none`. That converts "remember to update `RELATIONSHIPS`" into a gate, and puts the edge in the same diff as the DDL that creates the join.

Cost: one regex parser plus a convention to document, and the generator gains a second input directory. Judged worth surfacing because it retires the plan's only accepted-drift item at roughly the cost of the `RELATIONSHIPS` constant it replaces. Not applied — the operator decides whether it belongs in this task or a follow-up.
